---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Titanium Ring"
  category="Project | Product Design & Manufacturing"
  year="2026"
  description="Design and manufacturing of a one-piece open titanium ring featuring a central stone, polished surfaces and a gold-amber anodised finish."
>

  <p slot="intro">
    A personal product-design project focused on creating a one-piece open titanium ring,
    combining mechanical geometry, comfort, hand finishing and anodised colour treatment.
  </p>


  <!-- PROJECT SNAPSHOT -->

  <section class="portfolio-hero">

    <div class="portfolio-outcome">

      <span class="portfolio-kicker">
        Outcome
      </span>

      <h2>
        Turning a flat titanium sheet into a unique finished object.
      </h2>

      <p>
        The ring was designed from a single 2 mm titanium sheet,
        cut as one continuous piece before being progressively shaped into
        its final three-dimensional form.
      </p>

      <div class="portfolio-metrics">

        <div>
          <strong>2 mm</strong>
          <span>Titanium sheet thickness</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>One piece</strong>
          <span>Continuous ring architecture</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>OAS</strong>
          <span>Gold-amber anodised finish</span>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>


    <figure class="portfolio-main-visual">

      <img
        src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/ring-main.jpg"
        alt="Finished open titanium ring with central stone and gold anodised finish"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Final object | Polished titanium ring with gold-amber OAS finish
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 | CONTEXT -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      01
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Context
      </h2>

      <p>
        The project was developed as a personal gift rather than as a purely
        technical exercise.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective was to create a unique ring with a discreet visual identity,
        while keeping the design mechanically coherent and manufacturable from
        a single titanium blank.
      </p>

      <p>
        The final object therefore combines product design, jewellery-inspired
        geometry and small-scale manufacturing.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 | DESIGN INTENT -->

  <section class="portfolio-visual-section">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        02 | Design intent
      </span>

      <h2>
        Open by design.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="process-grid">

      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/initial-sketch.jpg"
          alt="Initial titanium ring sketch"
        />

        <figcaption>
          01 | Initial design exploration
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/flat-pattern.jpg"
          alt="Flat titanium ring pattern before bending"
        />

        <figcaption>
          02 | Flat geometry before forming
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/final-shape.jpg"
          alt="Final formed titanium ring"
        />

        <figcaption>
          03 | Final three-dimensional shape
        </figcaption>

      </figure>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 | DESIGN REQUIREMENTS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      03
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Design requirements
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Manufacture the ring from one continuous titanium piece
        </li>

        <li>
          Keep the ring intentionally open
        </li>

        <li>
          Integrate a central stone
        </li>

        <li>
          Maintain a discreet overall width
        </li>

        <li>
          Preserve a comfortable internal circular geometry
        </li>

        <li>
          Avoid sharp edges in contact with the skin
        </li>

        <li>
          Combine polished titanium with anodised colour
        </li>

        <li>
          Maintain a unique asymmetrical identity
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 | MY CONTRIBUTION -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      04
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        My contribution
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Developed the complete ring geometry
        </li>

        <li>
          Designed the flat pattern before forming
        </li>

        <li>
          Defined the open-ring architecture
        </li>

        <li>
          Integrated the central stone into the design
        </li>

        <li>
          Worked on comfort and internal geometry
        </li>

        <li>
          Manufactured and manually finished the part
        </li>

        <li>
          Applied polishing and anodised surface treatment
        </li>

        <li>
          Iterated the shape through physical evaluation
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 | ONE PIECE DESIGN -->

  <section class="portfolio-system">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        05 | One-piece design
      </span>

      <h2>
        Geometry before assembly.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="system-grid">

      <figure class="system-overview">

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/flat-pattern-detail.jpg"
          alt="Detailed flat geometry of the titanium ring"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Flat pattern | Entire ring cut from one titanium blank
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <div class="subsystem-stack">

        <figure>

          <img
            src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/formed-detail.jpg"
            alt="Formed titanium ring detail"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Formed geometry | Continuous material path
          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <div class="subsystem-copy">

          <h3>
            Continuous structure
          </h3>

          <p>
            The ring was designed so that the visual branches, stone support
            and main body remain part of a single continuous piece of titanium.
          </p>

          <p>
            The final three-dimensional form therefore had to be anticipated
            from the original flat geometry before bending.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 | MATERIAL -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      06
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Titanium
      </h2>

      <p>
        Titanium was selected both for its mechanical characteristics and for
        the possibilities offered by its surface finishing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The 2 mm sheet provides enough material to create a rigid object while
        still allowing the geometry to be formed manually into the final ring shape.
      </p>

      <p>
        Titanium also makes it possible to obtain controlled colours through
        anodisation without adding a conventional paint layer.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 | ERGONOMICS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      07
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Comfort & geometry
      </h2>

      <p>
        The internal shape was designed around an approximately 20 mm opening,
        with attention paid to circularity and contact comfort.
      </p>

      <p>
        Internal edges were softened with radiused transitions in order to reduce
        sharp contact points against the skin.
      </p>

      <p>
        The open architecture also required careful control of the two ends so
        that they remain visually separated without creating uncomfortable
        interference.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 | FORMING -->

  <section class="portfolio-wide-visual">

    <figure>

      <img
        src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/forming-sequence.jpg"
        alt="Titanium ring forming sequence from flat blank to final ring"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Manufacturing sequence | Flat blank → progressive forming → final geometry
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 09 | STONE -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      09
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Stone integration
      </h2>

      <p>
        The central stone became the visual focal point of the ring.
      </p>

      <p>
        Its integration influenced the surrounding geometry, particularly the
        two branches approaching the stone and the length of the descending arm.
      </p>

      <p>
        Several design iterations were required to preserve the open character
        of the ring while maintaining a balanced composition around the stone.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 10 | SURFACE FINISH -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      10
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Surface finish
      </h2>

      <p>
        The titanium surface was polished before the final colour treatment.
      </p>

      <p>
        An OAS anodising process was then used to create a gold tone with a
        subtle amber character.
      </p>

      <p>
        The combination of polished surfaces, titanium reflections and anodised
        colour was used to create a finish that remains visually distinctive
        without becoming overly decorative.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 11 | COLOUR STUDY -->

  <section class="portfolio-visual-section">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        11 | Finish study
      </span>

      <h2>
        Material becomes colour.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="process-grid">

      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/raw-titanium.jpg"
          alt="Raw titanium ring finish"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Raw titanium
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/polished.jpg"
          alt="Polished titanium ring"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Polished surface
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/titanium-ring/anodised-gold.jpg"
          alt="Gold anodised titanium ring"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Gold-amber OAS finish
        </figcaption>

      </figure>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 12 | ITERATION -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      12
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Design iteration
      </h2>

      <p>
        The ring evolved through several geometric iterations before reaching
        its final proportions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Particular attention was given to the length of the branch descending
        beside the stone, the internal circularity and the fact that the ring
        must remain visibly open.
      </p>

      <p>
        Physical forming revealed details that were difficult to predict from
        the flat pattern alone, making manual iteration an important part of
        the design process.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 13 | DOCUMENTATION -->

  <section class="portfolio-documents">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        13 | Documentation
      </span>

      <h2>
        Design records.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="document-grid">

      <a
        href="/documents/titanium-ring/design-study.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Design study
          </h3>

          <p>
            Design iterations, dimensions and development of the ring geometry.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>


      <a
        href="/documents/titanium-ring/flat-pattern.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Flat pattern
          </h3>

          <p>
            Two-dimensional geometry used before forming the titanium sheet.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>


      <a
        href="/documents/titanium-ring/finish-study.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Finish study
          </h3>

          <p>
            Surface preparation, polishing and anodised colour exploration.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 14 | SKILLS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      14
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Skills developed
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Product design
        </li>

        <li>
          Sheet-metal geometry
        </li>

        <li>
          Manual forming
        </li>

        <li>
          Titanium finishing
        </li>

        <li>
          Surface polishing
        </li>

        <li>
          Anodising
        </li>

        <li>
          Design iteration
        </li>

        <li>
          Detail-oriented manufacturing
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 15 | LEARNING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      15
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        What I learned
      </h2>

      <p>
        The Titanium Ring project showed me how strongly manufacturing influences
        the final perception of a product.
      </p>

      <p>
        Small differences in geometry, edge treatment, surface preparation and
        colour can completely change the quality of a finished object.
      </p>

      <p>
        It also gave me an opportunity to apply mechanical-design methods to a
        much smaller and more personal object, where aesthetics and emotion matter
        as much as technical function.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>

</ProjectLayout>